---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import FactoryTitle from './FactoryTitle.astro';
import FactoryMotionControl from './FactoryMotionControl.astro';
import AgentKanban from './AgentKanban.astro';
import RalphHeading from './RalphHeading.astro';
import slate from './assets/slate.png';
import codemill from './assets/codemill.png';
import kanbanMeme from './assets/slaps-roof-of-kanban.png';
import metadata from './metadata';
---
<ArticleLayout metadata={metadata} showDescription={false} dateBeforeTitle seamlessHeading>
  <FactoryTitle slot="title">
    <Fragment slot="intro">
      <p>The other day at <a href="https://www.thinkmill.com.au">Thinkmill</a> I demoed to the team a software factory I've been building
        for my personal apps and agent runtime. Having now built a whole one of them, with a haughty
        confidence I felt qualified to define the whole category.</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>Autonomous agents, orchestrated into a team's development workflow.</p>
      </blockquote>
    </Fragment>
  </FactoryTitle>
  <FactoryMotionControl slot="heading-controls" />

  <p>But I was picturing Kanbans, Jiras, Agiles, and Agents. Big team stuff.</p>

  <p>While I was building this factory for myself, I always had larger engineering
    teams in mind. Bringing agents into our existing workflows, one at a
    time, and deciding where humans stay in the loop.</p>

  <AgentKanban />

  <RalphHeading />

  <p>Within my own dev-loop, I'd been trialling different ways to
    bring agents into my workflow. Markdown files for epics with each task
    defined, <a href="https://ghuntley.com/loop/">Ralph loops</a> working through
    them. Switching between harnesses, from IDE to terminal to desktop apps,
    often juggling multiple sessions at a time.</p>

  <p>That was fine for sequential tasks but the more I tried to parallelise, the
    larger the mental overhead. Things reached a head on a project where I was
    juggling five-plus tasks at a time for weeks on end. Each had its own agent
    coding part, agent review, my review and team review feedback loop to keep
    track of. One of the agents ran locally, some were cloud based, there was a lot to keep track.</p>

  <p>I had notes for each ticket to remember where it was up to. I even had a
    Trello board at one point, but I was managing all of it manually, which was a
    huge PIA and exhausting.</p>

  <h2>The Kanban strikes back</h2>

  <p>I wanted a better more managed way of managing these agents and couldn't get the idea of
    the kanban board out of my head. I've ran software delivery teams for
    decades with a kanban so it felt natural to start there as the UX for my own software factory.</p>

  <p>This isn't particularly novel. Many software factories connect to Jira or Linear or have their own boards and backlogs to help manage work.
    But integrating it myself really showed the unlock of such a model, it felt like a leap from trying to manage everything locally, or via plain sessions
    with cloud-based agents.
  </p>

  <p>I plan with a planning agent that explores the repo and creates tickets and epics
    once we're happy with the plan. I assign a ticket to a coding agent and it
    works through coding before handing it off to the review agent. By the time
    it comes to me, either the agents are at an impasse or it's ready for my
    final review.</p>
  
  <p>Each agent has their own specific instructions permissions and tools.</p>

  <figure class="screenshot-figure">
    <a class="screenshot-image" href={slate.src} aria-label="View the full-size Slate screenshot">
      <Image src={slate}
        widths={[480, 896, 1792]}
        sizes="(min-width: 60.4rem) 50.4rem, 84vw"
        alt="Slate's kanban board, with Flintstones character avatars assigned to tasks across Backlog, Todo, In Progress, Review and Done."
        loading="lazy" />
    </a>
    <figcaption>I lent into a Flintstones theme for my factory ao I can yabba-dabba-doo
      my way through the software development process.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>One lesson I learned early was that observability is key. I needed to know
    what an agent was up to, including its token usage and tool calls. Those
    were things that mattered to me, so I built the agent-runner and factory around them.</p>

  <h2>BYO</h2>

  <p>So its simple, slap some agents into a Kanban, you got yourself a software
    factory bucko.</p>

  <figure class="kanban-meme">
    <div class="meme-image">
      <Image src={kanbanMeme}
        widths={[480, 896, 1424]}
        sizes="(min-width: 36rem) 30.24rem, 84vw"
        alt="Car salesman gestures at a kanban board over the car window. Caption: car salesman: *slaps roof of kanban* this bad boy can fit so many agents in it"
        loading="lazy" />
    </div>
  </figure>

  <p>Or not. After the talk, other Thinkmillers started sharing how they were
    improving automation on their own projects, from connecting project tracking
    to their agents to improving tool call outputs. There were Slack bots making
    code changes to blog articles, skipping the need for a CMS. Suddenly it was
    hard to draw the line on what makes a factory and what doesn't.</p>

  <p>Devs on X can't also can't agree: maybe Software Factory is cloud, it's
    recoverability, it's observability, it's no humans in the loop, it's lots of
    humans in the loop, it comes to you, we build it for you, you should build it yourself,{' '}
    <a href="https://x.com/mattpocockuk/status/2119748545691402733">Software
      Factories are bullshit</a>.</p>

  <p>Seeing other examples of what the team had build evolved my picture of a software factory to cover all these ways of
    automating parts of software development with agents. It can be big or small, for an
    individual or a whole team, it could be in the cloud, it could even still be
    local.</p>

  <p>The important part is giving agents more autonomy, and freeing
    ourselves from the grind of managing too many individual agents across too many sessions 
    with too much context. Maybe Software Factory is just a clunky term for the next
    evolution of how we develop software.</p>

  <p>I still like my kanban. It fits the way I work and solves a problem I was
    having (though the tools I'm building for Thinkmill's Software Factory have
    me using it less...). But an automated Slack to blog post CMS pipeline?
    It's SDLC, it's agent automation, it keeps humans in the loop when needed, but
    you just might not need a kanban.</p>

  <figure class="screenshot-figure codemill-figure">
    <a class="screenshot-image" href={codemill.src} aria-label="View the full-size Codemill screenshot">
      <Image src={codemill}
        widths={[480, 896, 1792]}
        sizes="(min-width: 60.4rem) 50.4rem, 84vw"
        alt="Codemill's dashboard showing planning, coding and review agents, their activity, tasks in flight and a factory console."
        loading="lazy" />
    </a>
    <figcaption>We're cookin' up some nice stuff over at Thinkmill HQ.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Like building a design system or running a software project, there isn't a
    one-size-fits-all solution when it comes to agent automation and software
    factories. We each have our own tools, project constraints and ways of working.</p>

  <p>In the end a software factory is what works for you (as well as the friends we make along
    the way).</p>

  <hr />

  <p><em>Looking to bring agents into your team's workflow? Build your own
    Software Factory? At <a href="https://www.thinkmill.com.au/">Thinkmill</a>,
    we'll help you work out where they can be useful and fit the approach to
    your project and team with your own tools.</em></p>
</ArticleLayout>

<style>
  .screenshot-figure, .kanban-meme { margin: var(--space-4) 0; }

  .kanban-meme {
    width: min(100%, calc(var(--width-site) * .6));
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .screenshot-image, .meme-image {
    display: block;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 3px solid var(--color-ink);
    border-radius: .75rem;
    background: var(--color-surface);
    box-shadow: 4px 4px 0 var(--color-ink);
  }

  .screenshot-image { cursor: zoom-in; }
  .screenshot-image img, .meme-image img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }
  .screenshot-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    line-height: 1.6;
    font-style: italic;
  }
</style>
